1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,050
Listen to a talk in a literature class.

2
00:00:04,050 --> 00:00:09,079
Magical realism is a style of fiction that combines realistic narrative

3
00:00:09,079 --> 00:00:12,280
with surreal elements of magic and fantasy.

4
00:00:12,280 --> 00:00:18,050
Unlike pure fantasy, magical realism is set in a realistic world.

5
00:00:18,050 --> 00:00:20,940
But extraordinary events occur without explanation

6
00:00:21,360 --> 00:00:23,250
blending the ordinary with the extraordinary.

7
00:00:23,250 --> 00:00:32,009
One of the most famous examples of magical realism is Gabriel García Márquez's novel "One Hundred Years of Solitude."

8
00:00:32,009 --> 00:00:36,745
In this book, everyday life in the fictional town of Macondo is interwoven

9
00:00:36,745 --> 00:00:42,210
with fantastic occurrences such as a character ascending to the heavens while hanging out laundry.

10
00:00:42,210 --> 00:00:48,010
These magical events are presented in a matter-of-fact manner, as if this is the way things should be.

11
00:00:48,010 --> 00:00:50,080
And this is a hallmark of the genre.

12
00:00:50,080 --> 00:00:54,824
Magical realism often serves to highlight complex social and cultural issues,

13
00:00:54,824 --> 00:01:00,000
offering a unique way to explore themes like identity, history and politics.

14
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:04,713
The blending of reality and magic allows authors to delve into deeper truths

15
00:01:04,713 --> 00:01:07,250
and challenge readers' perceptions of the world.

16
00:01:07,250 --> 00:01:13,150
Next, we will examine how magical realism has influenced other literary traditions

17
00:01:13,150 --> 00:01:16,100
and its role in contemporary literature.
